WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.880
A nucleotide is costly for a cell to build.

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:12.540
Nucleotides are the letters of DNA and RNA, and the carriers of energy, ATP and GTP.

3
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.840
They form the cores of signaling molecules like cyclic A M P. They also form the

4
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:24.060
backbones of cofactors such as NAD +, FAD, and coenzyme A. Building their rings from

5
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.660
scratch costs a small fortune in ATP.

6
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.360
So the cell recycles much of what it has.

7
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:34.850
There are two routes to purine nucleotide, and the difference is mostly about cost.

8
00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:40.210
De novo synthesis builds the purine ring atom by atom, directly onto an activated

9
00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:46.150
ribose called P R P P. It runs about ten enzymatic steps and spends roughly six ATP

10
00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:52.310
to make the first finished purine nucleotide, I M P. Both A M P and G M P branch

11
00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:53.310
from I M P.

12
00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:59.773
Salvage takes a free base that already exists, such as hypoxanthine, guanine, or

13
00:00:59.773 --> 00:01:03.413
adenine, and reattaches it to P R P P in a single step.

14
00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:08.013
The enzyme H G P R T salvages hypoxanthine and guanine.

15
00:01:08.653 --> 00:01:10.573
A P R T salvages adenine.

16
00:01:11.193 --> 00:01:15.333
Recycling is so much cheaper that many cells recover a large share of their free

17
00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:18.653
purines. Salvage is a major source in many tissues.

18
00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:21.947
Now imagine the recycling is gone.

19
00:01:22.587 --> 00:01:27.207
In Lesch-Nyhan syndrome, an X-linked defect, H G P R T is missing.

20
00:01:27.807 --> 00:01:31.947
Free bases that should have been reclaimed are dumped into the catabolic pathway and

21
00:01:31.947 --> 00:01:33.167
turned into uric acid.

22
00:01:33.787 --> 00:01:38.787
The P R P P that salvage would have used piles up and pushes de novo synthesis even

23
00:01:38.787 --> 00:01:41.747
higher. That makes still more purines to degrade.

24
00:01:42.347 --> 00:01:47.187
The result is severe hyperuricemia alongside profound neurological disease.

25
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.180
When a purine is finally retired, it is taken apart down a short tail.

26
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:56.180
A M P and adenosine funnel through inosine to hypoxanthine.

27
00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:01.720
Then the enzyme xanthine oxidase oxidizes hypoxanthine to xanthine, and xanthine to

28
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:06.120
uric acid. Guanine joins the route one step later, at xanthine.

29
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.760
Most mammals carry uricase, which converts uric acid into highly soluble allantoin.

30
00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.680
Humans and the other apes lost a working uricase during evolution.

31
00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.000
For us, uric acid is the end product, and it is only sparingly soluble.

32
00:02:22.083 --> 00:02:26.663
That missing enzyme makes us prone to gout, along with how well we excrete urate.

33
00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:31.663
Once serum urate climbs past roughly 6.8 milligrams per deciliter, it can

34
00:02:31.663 --> 00:02:33.723
crystallize as monosodium urate.

35
00:02:34.243 --> 00:02:37.243
A flare is the inflammation those needle-sharp crystals trigger.

36
00:02:37.903 --> 00:02:39.483
One fix is to make less of it.

37
00:02:40.163 --> 00:02:45.163
Allopurinol, a hypoxanthine look-alike, inhibits xanthine oxidase, so urate

38
00:02:45.163 --> 00:02:47.743
production falls and the crystals slowly dissolve.

39
00:02:48.863 --> 00:02:52.843
Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings designed it by reasoning about the enzyme.
